# =============================================================================
# 00 — Resource Group + shared conventions
# =============================================================================
#
# ⚠️  LEARNING REFERENCE — NOT run automatically by anyone/anything.
#     These are az CLI commands you read to understand provisioning, and run
#     yourself, line by line, ONLY when you've decided to. Nothing in this repo
#     executes them. They will create real, billable Azure resources when YOU
#     run them — see docs/PROVISIONING.md for the cost breakdown ($200 free
#     credit easily covers everything here; most of it is free-tier anyway).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THEORY — What is a Resource Group, and why start here?
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A Resource Group (RG) is a logical container that holds related Azure
# resources (your app, its database, its Key Vault, etc.) as a single unit.
# It is the single most important cost-control tool you have:
#   * Everything for one project goes in one RG.
#   * When you're done demoing/learning, you DELETE THE RG (see 99-teardown)
#     and every resource inside it is gone — billing stops instantly. This is
#     why you never have to worry about a forgotten Redis cache quietly eating
#     your credit for a month.
# An RG lives in a "location" (region), but resources inside it can be in other
# regions — the RG's own location just says where its metadata is stored.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Prerequisites (run once, on your own machine) ---------------------------
# Install the Azure CLI, then sign in. `az login` opens a browser and uses YOUR
# account — this is the only "credential" step; everything after authenticates
# through this session (the same DefaultAzureCredential chain the app uses
# locally — see docs/KEYVAULT.md).
#
#   az login
#   az account show            # confirm you're in the subscription you expect
#   az account set --subscription "<your-subscription-id-or-name>"

# --- Shared variables --------------------------------------------------------
# Bash-style variables. On PowerShell, use $RG = "..." etc. instead.
# Azure requires MANY resource names to be GLOBALLY unique (Key Vault, Storage,
# Web/Function App, Service Bus, Redis, Cosmos all share a global namespace).
# A short random suffix avoids "name already taken" errors.
RG="rg-highfidelity-learning"
LOCATION="eastus"                      # pick a region close to you; eastus is cheap and has every service
SUFFIX="$RANDOM"                       # e.g. 24917 — reused by later scripts for uniqueness
echo "Using suffix: $SUFFIX  (write this down — later scripts reuse it)"

# --- Create the resource group -----------------------------------------------
az group create \
  --name "$RG" \
  --location "$LOCATION"

# --- Verify ------------------------------------------------------------------
az group show --name "$RG" --output table

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT: run 01-keyvault.azcli. Each subsequent script assumes $RG, $LOCATION,
# and $SUFFIX are still set in your shell session. If you open a new terminal,
# re-set them at the top before continuing.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
